-base) along two axes — Standards (does the code follow this repo's documented coding standards?) and Spec (does the code match what the originating issue/PRD asked for?). Runs both reviews in parallel sub-agents and reports them side by side. Use when the user wants to review a branch, a PR, work-in-progress changes, or asks to \"review since X\".\n- codebase-design: Shared vocabulary for designing deep modules. Use when the user wants to design or improve a module's interface, find deepening opportunities, decide where a seam goes, make code more testable or AI-navigable, or when another skill needs the deep-module vocabulary.\n- codebase-exploration: Expert in Codebase Exploration and structural analysis. Trigger this skill when you need to navigate an unfamiliar codebase, map complex architectural dependencies, trace data flow across multiple files, or locate specific implementations of APIs and patterns. Essential for initial project onboarding and impact analysis. ALWAYS use multiple search tools (rg, fd, ast-grep) to cross-reference findings. NEVER start searching without establishing context and directory structure first.\n- cold-email\n- cold-email-copywriting\n- convert-documents-to-markdown\n- convex-advisor\n- convex-reviewer\n- convex-setup-auth\n- copy-editing\n- copywriting\n- create-cowork-plugin\n- database-schema-designer\n- dbt-data-transformation\n- diagnose\n- diagnosing-bugs\n- domain-modeling\n- evilmartians-ui: Builds HTML, React/JSX, and CSS components in the Evil Martians visual style — dark brutalist aesthetic, Martian Grotesk + Martian Mono fonts, OKLCH-based neon color system, per-card accent theming, EntityCard layout with cover images and two-tier tags, and developer-tool UX principles. Use this skill whenever the user asks to: build a UI, component, dashboard, devtool interface, landing page, or any visual artifact and (a) mentions Evil Martians style, (b) asks for a dark developer-tool aesthetic, or (c) has previously established this design system in the conversation. Also use when the user asks to make something \"look like imgproxy\", \"look like Polychrom\", or \"use the martian design system\". Trigger even if the request is casual like \"build me a settings panel\" — apply the full system proactively.\n- excalidraw-diagrams\n- excalidraw-visuals\n- feature-dev: Expert in end-to-end Feature Implementation. Trigger this skill whenever the user asks you to \"build a feature\", \"implement a system\", \"add functionality\", or perform full-cycle development from scratch. Use this for complex multi-step workflows that require systematic codebase exploration, architectural design, implementation, and automated testing. It will orchestrate subagents for discovery, architecture, and review. ALWAYS ask clarifying questions to identify edge cases and underspecified behaviors before acting. ALWAYS present all questions to the user and wait for answers before proceeding to architecture design. NEVER start implementation without explicit user approval of the proposed architecture. ALWAYS ensure all tests pass before proceeding to quality review.\n- ffmpeg\n- ffs: Capture what Claude just did wrong and turn it into a durable fix (CLAUDE.md rule, hook, permission, or memory). Trigger on /ffs with optional text describing the failure.\n- find-skills\n- firebase-basics\n- friction-guard\n- ftw\n- game-changing-features: Trigger this skill to aggressively identify high-leverage product features, brainstorm radical \"10x\" improvements, and align new feature ideas with strategic growth objectives. Use whenever the user wants to find game-changing moves that fundamentally expand the product's value proposition rather than just shipping incremental, nice-to-have features. Essential for pivoting a tool into a platform, unlocking new user adoption, or structurally overcoming competitive threats.\nALWAYS write outputs directly to `.claude/docs/ai/<product-or-area>/10x/session-N.md`. NEVER write code. NEVER outpu