{"parentUuid":"8c7a8a73-2d70-42ac-8045-d8c12c7d269e","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"hook_success","hookName":"UserPromptSubmit","toolUseID":"44447cc4-4a21-47a2-85b0-3942ed7f9166","hookEvent":"UserPromptSubmit","content":"[15:06 ACST -- jvent]","stdout":"[15:06 ACST -- jvent]\n","stderr":"","exitCode":0,"command":"bash \"${CLAUDE_PLUGIN_ROOT}/scripts/user-prompt-hook.sh\"","durationMs":23},"type":"attachment","uuid":"2f134ef8-1aed-4cdd-8c63-e5af71c2ff54","timestamp":"2026-09-09T05:36:38.331Z","rendered":[{"content":"<system-reminder>\nUserPromptSubmit hook success: [15:06 ACST -- jvent]\n</system-reminder>"}],"userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/jvent/Dev/supabase/supabase-devtool","sessionId":"21880ebb-4ca2-4b72-9b3d-3b6dba0010a6","version":"2.1.266","gitBranch":"main"}
{"parentUuid":"2f134ef8-1aed-4cdd-8c63-e5af71c2ff54","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"instructions","files":[{"path":"/Users/jvent/.claude/CLAUDE.md","type":"User","content":"# Coding Agent Session Guide\n\n## Package Manager\n\n**Default to Bun** (`bun`) — but check the lockfile first (`bun.lock`/`bun.lockb`, `pnpm-lock.yaml`, `package-lock.json`); many projects use pnpm or npm instead:\n- npm: `bun install` instead of `npm install`\n- npx: `bunx` instead of `npx`\n- Scripts: `bun run <script>` instead of `npm run <script>`\n\n## Your LLM Wiki\n\nA persistent knowledge base tracks all project work. You are expected to log significant activities.\n\n**Wiki Location**: `/Users/jvent/Dev/ai-agents-and-skills/llm-wiki`\n\n**Key Files**:\n- `log.md` — Append-only activity log (ingest, query, session summaries)\n- `index.md` — Content catalog (entities, concepts, sources, synthesis)\n- `AGENTS.md` — Full schema and conventions\n\n**Wiki Structure**:\n```\nllm-wiki/\n├── log.md                    # Activity log (append-only)\n├── index.md                  # Catalog of all pages\n├── wiki/\n│   ├── entities/             # People, organizations, projects\n│   ├── concepts/             # Patterns, methodologies, techniques\n│   ├── sources/              # Document summaries\n│   └── synthesis/            # Cross-cutting analyses\n```\n\n---\n\n## Session Logging Workflow\n\n### At Session Start\n- Read the last 40 lines of `log.md` (`tail -40`); the file is 250 KB+, never read it whole\n- Check `index.md` for relevant project context\n\n### During Session\n- Log significant actions to `log.md`:\n  ```\n  ## [YYYY-MM-DD] session | Project: Brief Description\n  - Completed: [what was done]\n  - Next: [what remains]\n  - Blockers: [if any]\n  ```\n\n### At Session End\n- Write a session summary to `log.md`\n- Update relevant entity/concept pages if new patterns discovered\n- Update `index.md` if new pages were created\n\n### Log Entry Format\n```markdown\n## [YYYY-MM-DD] session | Project Name — Brief description\n- Completed: [3-5 bullet points of significant work]\n- Decisions: [architectural choices, trade-offs made]\n- Next Steps: [immediate next actions]\n- Notes: [anything worth remembering]\n```\n\n---\n\n## Port Conflicts\n\nWhen a `dev` or `start` command fails with `EADDRINUSE` (port already in use), automatically retry on the next port without asking. Increment by 1 until a free port is found (e.g. 3000 → 3001 → 3002). Pass the port via the `-p` flag or `PORT` env var depending on the tool.\n\n---\n\n## Git Push Rules\n\nNever push directly to `main`/`master` — always work on a branch and open a PR,\neven if explicitly asked to push straight to main. Ask first if a push to\nmain seems necessary.\n\n---\n\n## Next.js 16 — Proxy (formerly Middleware)\n\nNext.js 16 renamed `middleware.ts` → `proxy.ts`. Key changes:\n\n### Migration\n- Rename `middleware.ts` → `proxy.ts`\n- Export: `middleware` → `proxy`\n- Config: `skipMiddlewareUrlNormalize` → `skipProxyUrlNormalize`\n- Codemod: `npx @next/codemod@canary middleware-to-proxy .`\n\n### Proxy Matcher Examples\n```ts\n// Basic\nmatcher: '/about/:path*'\n\n// Exclude internals\nmatcher: '/((?!_next/static|_next/image|favicon.ico).*)'\n\n// Protected paths\nmatcher: ['/dashboard/:path*', '/api/protected/:path*']\n```\n\n### Best Practices\n- Edge runtime only—keep lightweight\n- Use for routing/auth gating; verify data access in handlers\n- BFF/API proxying via rewrites\n\n## When to Log\n\n| Action | Log Type |\n|--------|----------|\n| Project setup complete | `session` |\n| Feature implemented | `session` |\n| Bug fixed (nontrivial) | `session` |\n| Architectural decision | `session` + concept page |\n| New project discovered | `session` + entity page |\n| Research/thinking | `query` or `session` |\n| Lint/health check | `lint` |\n\n---\n\n## Shell Command Rules (Token Hygiene)\n\nThese rules prevent context pollution from expensive shell patterns:\n\n- **NEVER** use `psql \"postgresql://...\"` — use `mcp__supabase__execute_sql` instead\n- **NEVER** use `curl -s http://localhost:*` — use WebFetch or the Supabase MCP tools\n- **NEVER** use `python3 -c \"...\"` inline — write to `/tmp/script.py` and execute that\n- **NEVER** use `node -e \"...\"` inline — write to `/tmp/script.mjs` and execute that\n- **NEVER** use heredoc scripts (`python3 << 'EOF'`) — write to file first, same as above\n\n**Why:** Inline scripts (128 `node -e`, 100 `python3 -c`, 70 heredocs) flood context with escaped strings. Each `psql` call (60) and `curl` call (64) bypasses MCP tooling and carries raw output into context. Use the right tool and the output is capped automatically.\n\n---\n\n## Browser Automation (Token Hygiene)\n\n**Default to the `agent-browser` CLI** (`/opt/homebrew/bin/agent-browser`, installed system-wide) over the `claude-in-chrome` / `chrome-devtools` MCP tools for ad-hoc browser verification (checking a deployed page, testing a login flow, confirming a UI fix rendered correctly).\n\n```bash\nagent-browser open <url>       # launch + navigate\nagent-browser snapshot         # accessibility tree with @refs — pipe through rg for just what you need\nagent-browser click @e1        # or a CSS/XPath selector\nagent-browser fill @e2 \"text\"\nagent-browser screenshot [path]\nagent-browser read             # rendered text of the active tab\nagent-browser close\n```\n\n**Why:** terser output than MCP round-trips (most commands return one line, e.g. `✓ Done`), no fixed schema-load cost per call, and snapshots can be filtered before they ever hit context (`agent-browser snapshot | rg -i button`). It also self-reports covering elements on a blocked click (`✗ Element covered by <a.absolute.inset-0>...`) which MCP click tools don't surface as cleanly.\n\n**Keep using the MCP browser tools when:** the task needs `claude-in-chrome`'s access to the user's *actual logged-in browser* (their real cookies/session, not a fresh automated one), or needs a capability `agent-browser` doesn't have (e.g. `chrome-devtools`'s performance tracing/Lighthouse).\n\n---\n\n## Conventions\n\n- Use `[[page-id|Display Text]]` for internal wiki links\n- Frontmatter required on new pages: `title`, `type`, `tags`, `created`, `updated`\n- Kebab-case filenames: `project-name.md`\n- Update `index.md` within 24hrs of creating new pages\n\n## Known Model Names (Non-Anthropic)\n\nReference list of model IDs from other providers:\n\n### Alibaba / Qwen\n- `qwen3.5`\n- `qwen3-coder-next`\n\n### Meta / Llama\n- `llama-4-scout`\n- `llama-3.1-70b`\n\n### DeepSeek\n- `deepseek-r1`\n- `deepseek-v3.2`\n\n### xAI / Grok\n- `grok-4.20`\n- `grok-4.2`\n\n### Moonshot / Kimi\n- `kimi-k2`\n- `kimi-k2.5`\n\n### Mistral\n- `mistral-large-2`\n- `mistral-7b`\n- `mixtral-8x7b`\n\n### Microsoft / Phi\n- `phi-3`\n- `phi-4`\n\n### Cohere\n- `command-r`\n\n### Amazon\n- `nova-pro`\n\n### OpenAI / GPT\n- `gpt-5.5` — flagship frontier\n- `gpt-5.5-pro`\n- `gpt-5.4` — coding/professional\n- `gpt-5.4-pro`\n- `gpt-5.4-mini` — default fast model\n- `gpt-5.4-nano`\n- `gpt-realtime-2`\n- `gpt-image-2`\n- `gpt-4o-transcribe`\n- `gpt-4o-mini-transcribe`\n\n---\n\n@RTK.md\n# graphify\n- **graphify** (`~/.claude/skills/graphify/SKILL.md`) - any input to knowledge graph. Trigger: `/graphify`\nWhen the user types `/graphify`, use the installed graphify skill or instructions before doing anything else.\n\n# unslop\n- **unslop** (`~/.claude/skills/unslop/SKILL.md`) - cut AI tells from writing. Trigger: `/unslop`\nApply the unslop rules to every response and every document you write, without being asked.\nNo em dashes. Say what a thing does, not how it feels. Cut filler, puffery, and hedging.\nRead the skill file for the full pattern list when writing anything longer than a few lines.\n\n# autonomy\nDefault to acting, not asking.\n\n- **Just fix it, no permission needed:** bugs found on the way to the real task,\n  anything you broke, anything that restores previously-working behaviour, cleanup\n  and dead files inside the repo, stale copies of things you just changed. Report it\n  after in one line, under a \"also fixed\" heading.\n- **Ask first, always:** deleting data, force-push, rotating credentials, anything\n  that costs money, anything irreversible outside the repo.\n- **Never end a message with \"want me to X?\"** If X is worth doing, do it now. If it\n  isn't, drop it silently. If it is genuinely Jesse's call, state your recommendation\n  and proceed on it unless told otherwise.\n- **One exception:** if two readings of the request produce materially different work,\n  ask once, before starting. Not at the end.\n- Noticing a problem is not a reason to raise it. Fix it, or let it go.\n\n# read-before-overwrite\nRead a file before overwriting it. `Write` on an existing path destroys what is there,\nand \"I am creating this file\" is a belief, not a fact — check it.\n\nTest files are where this bites. A route test named exactly what you'd name it already\nexists more often than not. I clobbered one this way and lost four cases; the only reason\nit surfaced was that the suite total rose by four when I had added eight.\n\n- Before `Write`, confirm the path is absent (`ls`, or a failed read).\n- After, `git status` should show `A`, not `M`. `M` on a file you meant to create means\n  you destroyed something — recover it with `git show HEAD:<path>` before doing anything\n  else, and merge rather than re-deciding what the old cases should have been.\n- Editing an existing file is `Edit`, always. Reach for `Write` only for a genuinely new path.\n\n# plan-first\nDefault to planning before building. For any task that touches more than one file,\nchanges behaviour, or has more than one reasonable approach: enter plan mode (EnterPlanMode),\nread the code the change touches, write the plan (files, steps, checks), then execute it.\nSkip the plan only for one-line fixes, typos, and pure questions. Never skip because the\ntask \"looks simple\". A UserPromptSubmit hook injects PLAN REQUIRED on non-trivial prompts; obey it.\n\n# artifacts\nNever create an Artifact (published HTML page) unless Jesse asks for one by name in the\nprompt. Reports, summaries, plans, and comparisons go in the terminal reply. A PreToolUse\nhook enforces this; do not try to route around it.\n\n# issues\nWhen Jesse says \"issues\" (outstanding, open, close out, triage), that means GitHub\nissues: run `gh issue list --state open` first and work from that list. Never treat\na local doc, plan, or gate file as the issue tracker. Ask only if `gh` returns nothing."},{"path":"/Users/jvent/.claude/RTK.md","type":"User","content":"# RTK - Rust Token Killer\n\n**Usage**: Token-optimized CLI proxy (60-90% savings on dev operations)\n\n## Meta Commands (always use rtk directly)\n\n```bash\nrtk gain              # Show token savings analytics\nrtk gain --history    # Show command usage history with savings\nrtk discover          # Analyze Claude Code history for missed opportunities\nrtk proxy <cmd>       # Execute raw command without filtering (for debugging)\n```\n\n## Installation Verification\n\n```bash\nrtk --version         # Should show: rtk X.Y.Z\nrtk gain              # Should work (not \"command not found\")\nwhich rtk             # Verify correct binary\n```\n\n⚠️ **Name collision**: If `rtk gain` fails, you may have reachingforthejack/rtk (Rust Type Kit) installed instead.\n\n## Hook-Based Usage\n\nAll other commands are automatically rewritten by the Claude Code hook.\nExample: `git status` → `rtk git status` (transparent, 0 tokens overhead)\n\nRefer to CLAUDE.md for full command reference."},{"path":"/Users/jvent/Dev/CLAUDE.md","type":"Project","content":"# CLAUDE.md\n\nThis file provides guidance to Claude Code (claude.ai/code) when working with code in this repository.\n\n## Workspace Overview\n\n`/Users/jvent/Dev` is a **multi-project workspace** (not a single codebase). Projects are prefixed numerically (`00-`, `01-`, etc.) for active projects. Archived or experimental work lives in `_archive/`, `_data/`, `_docs/`, and `_scripts/`.\n\nMost projects follow one of two patterns:\n- **Vite SPA** — React + TypeScript, Supabase backend, Tailwind + shadcn/ui\n- **Turborepo monorepo** — `apps/` + `packages/` structure, multiple frontends sharing a `packages/database`, `packages/ui`, `packages/ai`\n\n## Standard Stack\n\n- **Frontend:** React + TypeScript, Vite (SPAs) or Next.js App Router (full-stack)\n- **Styling:** Tailwind CSS + shadcn/ui\n- **State:** Zustand for global state\n- **Database:** Supabase (PostgreSQL + pgvector + Edge Functions)\n- **AI:** Google Gemini (2.0 Flash, 2.5 Pro)\n- **Monorepos:** Turborepo + pnpm workspaces\n- **Healthcare data:** DuckDB + OMOP CDM v5.4\n\n## Common Commands\n\nMost projects use one of these package managers — check the lockfile (`pnpm-lock.yaml`, `bun.lock`, `package-lock.json`). Supabase local dev: `supabase start` / `db push` / `functions serve`. Turborepo monorepos: `pnpm --filter <app-name> dev`, `pnpm turbo build`.\n\n## Code Conventions\n\n- **TypeScript:** 2-space indent, camelCase, no `any` — define interfaces for everything\n- **Python:** 4-space indent, snake_case, PEP 8\n- **Naming:** Capability-based, not abstract — `DocumentUpload` not `InspirationHub`\n- **Commits:** Conventional Commits (`feat:`, `fix:`, `chore:` etc.)\n- **No over-engineering:** Simple solutions, no premature abstractions, no gratuitous comments\n\n## Modern CLI Substitutions (Token Hygiene)\n\nPrefer these modern tools over legacy commands — their default output is leaner,\ncutting context tokens at the source. All are installed system-wide.\n\n| Prefer | Instead of | Notes |\n|--------|-----------|-------|\n| `rg`   | `grep`    | recursive **by default**; `rg -n pattern`; no `-r`/`--include` needed |\n| `fd`   | `find`    | regex first arg: `fd '\\.ts$'`; by ext: `fd -e ts`; no `-name`/`-type f` boilerplate |\n| `bat`  | `cat`     | `bat --plain file` for raw; but **prefer the Read tool** for full files |\n| `eza`  | `ls`      | `eza -la --git`; tree: `eza -T` |\n| `sd`   | `sed`     | `sd 'old' 'new' file` (regex); no `/` delimiter pain |\n| `dust` | `du`      | `dust -d 2` (depth); visual disk usage |\n| `duf`  | `df`      | clean table; `duf --only local` |\n| `procs`| `ps`      | `procs <name>` to filter; far less noise than `ps aux` |\n| `xh`   | `curl`    | ad-hoc HTTP test only — never localhost/internal; use WebFetch or MCP per rules above |\n| `btm`  | `top`     | **interactive only** — never inside the agent loop |\n| `z`    | `cd`      | zoxide frecency jump (init'd in shell); Bash tool uses absolute paths anyway |\n\n**Rules:**\n- Searching → `rg` / `fd`, never `grep` / `find`.\n- Reading a whole file → **Read tool first**, not `bat` / `cat`. `bat` only for a quick syntax-highlighted peek.\n- `btm` / `procs` / `dust` / `duf` are heavy or interactive — run once, never in tight loops.\n- `xh` is for manual HTTP debugging only. For URLs → WebFetch; for Supabase → MCP tools; for localhost → WebFetch (never raw `curl`).\n\n## Supabase Patterns\n\nMost projects use a consistent pattern:\n- `supabase/migrations/` — schema history (never edit old files)\n- `supabase/functions/` — Edge Functions (Deno runtime)\n- `src/lib/supabase.ts` — typed Supabase client\n- `src/lib/api.ts` — RPC wrappers and data access functions\n- Row Level Security enabled on all user-facing tables\n\n## Turborepo Monorepo Structure\n\n```\napps/\n  dashboard/    # Admin/creator interface (Vite)\n  marketplace/  # Public-facing (Next.js)\npackages/\n  ai/           # Gemini/LLM integration\n  database/     # Supabase client + generated types\n  ui/           # Shared shadcn/ui components\nsupabase/\n  migrations/\n  functions/\n```\n\n## Scaffolds & Scripts\n\n- `scaffolds/` — reusable project templates\n- `_scripts/` — workspace-level utilities (`repo_analyzer.sh`, `code_analyzer.sh`, `build_call_graph.py`)\n- `_docs/` — cross-project documentation and analysis outputs"},{"path":"/Users/jvent/Dev/supabase/supabase-devtool/CLAUDE.md","type":"Project","content":"# CLAUDE.md\n\nThis file provides guidance to Claude Code (claude.ai/code) when working with code in this repository.\n\n## What This Is\n\nA Supabase developer tool built with Next.js 16 App Router. Connect to multiple Supabase projects and inspect schema, RLS policies, edge functions, run SQL, view indexes/triggers, profile tables, and more. Has a Demo Mode that works without a real connection.\n\n## Commands\n\n```bash\n# Dev server (Bun, port 3000)\nbun dev\n\n# Build (outputs standalone bundle)\nbun run build\n\n# Production start\nbun start\n\n# Lint\nbun run lint\n```\n\nNo environment variables are required to run the app. There is no local database — no Prisma, no SQLite. See `.env.example` for optional config (LLM API keys, S3/Iceberg pre-fill).\n\n## Architecture\n\n**Single-page shell + dynamic panels.** `src/app/page.tsx` is the main shell — it manages navigation, connection selection, and renders the active panel. All feature panels (`src/components/*.tsx`) are dynamically imported with `next/dynamic` for lazy loading.\n\n**API routes** (`src/app/api/`) are the backend. Each route reads connection credentials directly from the request body and proxies calls to Supabase. There is no server-side database — credentials come from the client on each request.\n\n**API route map:**\n- `agent/chat/` — server proxy for LLM calls (OpenAI / Anthropic / Google); injects API key server-side\n- `catalog/setup` — creates `catalog_tables` + `catalog_columns` tables in the connected project\n- `catalog/profile` — runs data profiling queries (row counts, null %, distinct, min/max)\n- `catalog/load` — retrieves stored catalog metadata\n- `catalog/commit` — saves AI-generated descriptions back to catalog\n- `connections/` — CRUD for stored connections (credentials validated client-side)\n- `connections/[id]/health` — four-point health check (URL, anon key, access token, service role key)\n- `schema/` — introspects tables, columns, foreign keys (Management API SQL → PostgREST OpenAPI → REST discovery)\n- `rls/` — RLS enable status + policies\n- `sql/` — execute arbitrary SQL via Management API\n- `tables/rows/` — fetch paginated row data via PostgREST (prefers `serviceRoleKey` to bypass RLS)\n- `edge-functions/` + `invoke/` — list and call edge functions\n- `database/indexes/`, `triggers/`, `views-functions/` — Management API SQL introspection\n- `project/` — project metadata\n- `realtime-token/` — generate a Realtime WebSocket auth token\n- `storage/` — bucket/folder browsing via Supabase Storage API\n- `storage/download/` — proxy for file download\n\n**State** is managed by two Zustand stores, both with `persist` middleware to `localStorage`:\n- `src/store/supabase-store.ts` (key: `supabase-debug-storage`) — connections, activeConnectionId, activePanel, SQL history, schema snapshots, migration history, latency records. Transient state (loaded tables, RLS statuses, loading/error flags) is not persisted.\n- `src/store/agent-store.ts` — LLM provider config (provider, model, baseURL, apiKey), maxSteps. Transient: messages, currentTask, agentStatus.\n\n**Two Supabase auth paths:**\n- Management API (`https://api.supabase.com/v1/projects/{ref}/...`) — requires `accessToken` (`sbp_...`). Used for schema introspection, SQL execution, RLS queries, data catalog profiling.\n- PostgREST (`{supabaseUrl}/rest/v1/...`) — requires `serviceRoleKey` or `anonKey`. New opaque key formats (`sb_secret_`, `sb_publishable_`) are exchanged for JWTs via `/auth/v1/token?grant_type=anonymous` before use. See `getValidApiKey()` in `supabase-helpers.ts`.\n\n**AI Agent** (`src/agent/`) — a PageAgent that autonomously queries schemas, runs SQL, inspects RLS, and debugs across multiple steps. Tools are defined in `supabase-tools.ts`. LLM calls are proxied through `/api/agent/chat` so API keys never reach the browser.\n\n**Types** are centralized in `src/lib/supabase-types.ts`. Import all shared interfaces from there.\n\n## Key Files\n\n| File | Purpose |\n|------|---------|\n| `src/app/page.tsx` | Main shell, nav, connection management UI |\n| `src/store/supabase-store.ts` | Main Zustand store (connections, SQL history, snapshots, latency) |\n| `src/store/agent-store.ts` | Agent Zustand store (LLM config, messages) |\n| `src/lib/supabase-types.ts` | All shared TypeScript interfaces |\n| `src/lib/supabase-helpers.ts` | Server-side functions calling Supabase APIs |\n| `src/lib/supabase-client.ts` | Client-side `createClient` factory |\n| `src/lib/demo-data.ts` | Mock data for Demo Mode |\n| `src/agent/supabase-tools.ts` | Agent tool definitions |\n| `src/agent/use-devtool-agent.ts` | React hook managing PageAgent lifecycle |\n\n## Notes\n\n- `next.config.ts` has `typescript: { ignoreBuildErrors: true }` — TypeScript errors won't fail the build, but type correctness still matters for maintainability.\n- ESLint is configured very permissively (most rules disabled) — don't rely on lint to catch issues.\n- `reactStrictMode: false` — effects run once in dev.\n- The `examples/` directory contains development task prompts/worklog artifacts, not source code.\n- There is no Prisma, no SQLite, no `db/` directory. All persistence is `localStorage` only."}]},"type":"attachment","uuid":"2120ac77-e2e2-48da-8e56-c10fb5b06ba7","timestamp":"2026-09-09T05:36:39.586Z","rendered":[{"content":"<system-reminder>\nCodebase and user instructions are shown below. Be sure to adhere to these instructions. IMPORTANT: These instructions OVERRIDE any default behavior and you MUST follow them exactly as written.\n\nContents of /Users/jvent/.claude/CLAUDE.md (user's private global instructions for all projects):\n\n# Coding Agent Session Guide\n\n## Package Manager\n\n**Default to Bun** (`bun`) — but check the lockfile first (`bun.lock`/`bun.lockb`, `pnpm-lock.yaml`, `package-lock.json`); many projects use pnpm or npm instead:\n- npm: `bun install` instead of `npm install`\n- npx: `bunx` instead of `npx`\n- Scripts: `bun run <script>` instead of `npm run <script>`\n\n## Your LLM Wiki\n\nA persistent knowledge base tracks all project work. You are expected to log significant activities.\n\n**Wiki Location**: `/Users/jvent/Dev/ai-agents-and-skills/llm-wiki`\n\n**Key Files**:\n- `log.md` — Append-only activity log (ingest, query, session summaries)\n- `index.md` — Content catalog (entities, concepts, sources, synthesis)\n- `AGENTS.md` — Full schema and conventions\n\n**Wiki Structure**:\n```\nllm-wiki/\n├── log.md                    # Activity log (append-only)\n├── index.md                  # Catalog of all pages\n├── wiki/\n│   ├── entities/             # People, organizations, projects\n│   ├── concepts/             # Patterns, methodologies, techniques\n│   ├── sources/              # Document summaries\n│   └── synthesis/            # Cross-cutting analyses\n```\n\n---\n\n## Session Logging Workflow\n\n### At Session Start\n- Read the last 40 lines of `log.md` (`tail -40`); the file is 250 KB+, never read it whole\n- Check `index.md` for relevant project context\n\n### During Session\n- Log significant actions to `log.md`:\n  ```\n  ## [YYYY-MM-DD] session | Project: Brief Description\n  - Completed: [what was done]\n  - Next: [what remains]\n  - Blockers: [if any]\n  ```\n\n### At Session End\n- Write a session summary to `log.md`\n- Update relevant entity/concept pages if new patterns discovered\n- Update `index.md` if new pages were created\n\n### Log Entry Format\n```markdown\n## [YYYY-MM-DD] session | Project Name — Brief description\n- Completed: [3-5 bullet points of significant work]\n- Decisions: [architectural choices, trade-offs made]\n- Next Steps: [immediate next actions]\n- Notes: [anything worth remembering]\n```\n\n---\n\n## Port Conflicts\n\nWhen a `dev` or `start` command fails with `EADDRINUSE` (port already in use), automatically retry on the next port without asking. Increment by 1 until a free port is found (e.g. 3000 → 3001 → 3002). Pass the port via the `-p` flag or `PORT` env var depending on the tool.\n\n---\n\n## Git Push Rules\n\nNever push directly to `main`/`master` — always work on a branch and open a PR,\neven if explicitly asked to push straight to main. Ask first if a push to\nmain seems necessary.\n\n---\n\n## Next.js 16 — Proxy (formerly Middleware)\n\nNext.js 16 renamed `middleware.ts` → `proxy.ts`. Key changes:\n\n### Migration\n- Rename `middleware.ts` → `proxy.ts`\n- Export: `middleware` → `proxy`\n- Config: `skipMiddlewareUrlNormalize` → `skipProxyUrlNormalize`\n- Codemod: `npx @next/codemod@canary middleware-to-proxy .`\n\n### Proxy Matcher Examples\n```ts\n// Basic\nmatcher: '/about/:path*'\n\n// Exclude internals\nmatcher: '/((?!_next/static|_next/image|favicon.ico).*)'\n\n// Protected paths\nmatcher: ['/dashboard/:path*', '/api/protected/:path*']\n```\n\n### Best Practices\n- Edge runtime only—keep lightweight\n- Use for routing/auth gating; verify data access in handlers\n- BFF/API proxying via rewrites\n\n## When to Log\n\n| Action | Log Type |\n|--------|----------|\n| Project setup complete | `session` |\n| Feature implemented | `session` |\n| Bug fixed (nontrivial) | `session` |\n| Architectural decision | `session` + concept page |\n| New project discovered | `session` + entity page |\n| Research/thinking | `query` or `session` |\n| Lint/health check | `lint` |\n\n---\n\n## Shell Command Rules (Token Hygiene)\n\nThese rules prevent context pollution from expensive shell patterns:\n\n- **NEVER** use `psql \"postgresql://...\"` — use `mcp__supabase__execute_sql` instead\n- **NEVER** use `curl -s http://localhost:*` — use WebFetch or the Supabase MCP tools\n- **NEVER** use `python3 -c \"...\"` inline — write to `/tmp/script.py` and execute that\n- **NEVER** use `node -e \"...\"` inline — write to `/tmp/script.mjs` and execute that\n- **NEVER** use heredoc scripts (`python3 << 'EOF'`) — write to file first, same as above\n\n**Why:** Inline scripts (128 `node -e`, 100 `python3 -c`, 70 heredocs) flood context with escaped strings. Each `psql` call (60) and `curl` call (64) bypasses MCP tooling and carries raw output into context. Use the right tool and the output is capped automatically.\n\n---\n\n## Browser Automation (Token Hygiene)\n\n**Default to the `agent-browser` CLI** (`/opt/homebrew/bin/agent-browser`, installed system-wide) over the `claude-in-chrome` / `chrome-devtools` MCP tools for ad-hoc browser verification (checking a deployed page, testing a login flow, confirming a UI fix rendered correctly).\n\n```bash\nagent-browser open <url>       # launch + navigate\nagent-browser snapshot         # accessibility tree with @refs — pipe through rg for just what you need\nagent-browser click @e1        # or a CSS/XPath selector\nagent-browser fill @e2 \"text\"\nagent-browser screenshot [path]\nagent-browser read             # rendered text of the active tab\nagent-browser close\n```\n\n**Why:** terser output than MCP round-trips (most commands return one line, e.g. `✓ Done`), no fixed schema-load cost per call, and snapshots can be filtered before they ever hit context (`agent-browser snapshot | rg -i button`). It also self-reports covering elements on a blocked click (`✗ Element covered by <a.absolute.inset-0>...`) which MCP click tools don't surface as cleanly.\n\n**Keep using the MCP browser tools when:** the task needs `claude-in-chrome`'s access to the user's *actual logged-in browser* (their real cookies/session, not a fresh automated one), or needs a capability `agent-browser` doesn't have (e.g. `chrome-devtools`'s performance tracing/Lighthouse).\n\n---\n\n## Conventions\n\n- Use `[[page-id|Display Text]]` for internal wiki links\n- Frontmatter required on new pages: `title`, `type`, `tags`, `created`, `updated`\n- Kebab-case filenames: `project-name.md`\n- Update `index.md` within 24hrs of creating new pages\n\n## Known Model Names (Non-Anthropic)\n\nReference list of model IDs from other providers:\n\n### Alibaba / Qwen\n- `qwen3.5`\n- `qwen3-coder-next`\n\n### Meta / Llama\n- `llama-4-scout`\n- `llama-3.1-70b`\n\n### DeepSeek\n- `deepseek-r1`\n- `deepseek-v3.2`\n\n### xAI / Grok\n- `grok-4.20`\n- `grok-4.2`\n\n### Moonshot / Kimi\n- `kimi-k2`\n- `kimi-k2.5`\n\n### Mistral\n- `mistral-large-2`\n- `mistral-7b`\n- `mixtral-8x7b`\n\n### Microsoft / Phi\n- `phi-3`\n- `phi-4`\n\n### Cohere\n- `command-r`\n\n### Amazon\n- `nova-pro`\n\n### OpenAI / GPT\n- `gpt-5.5` — flagship frontier\n- `gpt-5.5-pro`\n- `gpt-5.4` — coding/professional\n- `gpt-5.4-pro`\n- `gpt-5.4-mini` — default fast model\n- `gpt-5.4-nano`\n- `gpt-realtime-2`\n- `gpt-image-2`\n- `gpt-4o-transcribe`\n- `gpt-4o-mini-transcribe`\n\n---\n\n@RTK.md\n# graphify\n- **graphify** (`~/.claude/skills/graphify/SKILL.md`) - any input to knowledge graph. Trigger: `/graphify`\nWhen the user types `/graphify`, use the installed graphify skill or instructions before doing anything else.\n\n# unslop\n- **unslop** (`~/.claude/skills/unslop/SKILL.md`) - cut AI tells from writing. Trigger: `/unslop`\nApply the unslop rules to every response and every document you write, without being asked.\nNo em dashes. Say what a thing does, not how it feels. Cut filler, puffery, and hedging.\nRead the skill file for the full pattern list when writing anything longer than a few lines.\n\n# autonomy\nDefault to acting, not asking.\n\n- **Just fix it, no permission needed:** bugs found on the way to the real task,\n  anything you broke, anything that restores previously-working behaviour, cleanup\n  and dead files inside the repo, stale copies of things you just changed. Report it\n  after in one line, under a \"also fixed\" heading.\n- **Ask first, always:** deleting data, force-push, rotating credentials, anything\n  that costs money, anything irreversible outside the repo.\n- **Never end a message with \"want me to X?\"** If X is worth doing, do it now. If it\n  isn't, drop it silently. If it is genuinely Jesse's call, state your recommendation\n  and proceed on it unless told otherwise.\n- **One exception:** if two readings of the request produce materially different work,\n  ask once, before starting. Not at the end.\n- Noticing a problem is not a reason to raise it. Fix it, or let it go.\n\n# read-before-overwrite\nRead a file before overwriting it. `Write` on an existing path destroys what is there,\nand \"I am creating this file\" is a belief, not a fact — check it.\n\nTest files are where this bites. A route test named exactly what you'd name it already\nexists more often than not. I clobbered one this way and lost four cases; the only reason\nit surfaced was that the suite total rose by four when I had added eight.\n\n- Before `Write`, confirm the path is absent (`ls`, or a failed read).\n- After, `git status` should show `A`, not `M`. `M` on a file you meant to create means\n  you destroyed something — recover it with `git show HEAD:<path>` before doing anything\n  else, and merge rather than re-deciding what the old cases should have been.\n- Editing an existing file is `Edit`, always. Reach for `Write` only for a genuinely new path.\n\n# plan-first\nDefault to planning before building. For any task that touches more than one file,\nchanges behaviour, or has more than one reasonable approach: enter plan mode (EnterPlanMode),\nread the code the change touches, write the plan (files, steps, checks), then execute it.\nSkip the plan only for one-line fixes, typos, and pure questions. Never skip because the\ntask \"looks simple\". A UserPromptSubmit hook injects PLAN REQUIRED on non-trivial prompts; obey it.\n\n# artifacts\nNever create an Artifact (published HTML page) unless Jesse asks for one by name in the\nprompt. Reports, summaries, plans, and comparisons go in the terminal reply. A PreToolUse\nhook enforces this; do not try to route around it.\n\n# issues\nWhen Jesse says \"issues\" (outstanding, open, close out, triage), that means GitHub\nissues: run `gh issue list --state open` first and work from that list. Never treat\na local doc, plan, or gate file as the issue tracker. Ask only if `gh` returns nothing.\n\nContents of /Users/jvent/.claude/RTK.md (user's private global instructions for all projects):\n\n# RTK - Rust Token Killer\n\n**Usage**: Token-optimized CLI proxy (60-90% savings on dev operations)\n\n## Meta Commands (always use rtk directly)\n\n```bash\nrtk gain              # Show token savings analytics\nrtk gain --history    # Show command usage history with savings\nrtk discover          # Analyze Claude Code history for missed opportunities\nrtk proxy <cmd>       # Execute raw command without filtering (for debugging)\n```\n\n## Installation Verification\n\n```bash\nrtk --version         # Should show: rtk X.Y.Z\nrtk gain              # Should work (not \"command not found\")\nwhich rtk             # Verify correct binary\n```\n\n⚠️ **Name collision**: If `rtk gain` fails, you may have reachingforthejack/rtk (Rust Type Kit) installed instead.\n\n## Hook-Based Usage\n\nAll other commands are automatically rewritten by the Claude Code hook.\nExample: `git status` → `rtk git status` (transparent, 0 tokens overhead)\n\nRefer to CLAUDE.md for full command reference.\n\nContents of /Users/jvent/Dev/CLAUDE.md (project instructions, checked into the codebase):\n\n# CLAUDE.md\n\nThis file provides guidance to Claude Code (claude.ai/code) when working with code in this repository.\n\n## Workspace Overview\n\n`/Users/jvent/Dev` is a **multi-project workspace** (not a single codebase). Projects are prefixed numerically (`00-`, `01-`, etc.) for active projects. Archived or experimental work lives in `_archive/`, `_data/`, `_docs/`, and `_scripts/`.\n\nMost projects follow one of two patterns:\n- **Vite SPA** — React + TypeScript, Supabase backend, Tailwind + shadcn/ui\n- **Turborepo monorepo** — `apps/` + `packages/` structure, multiple frontends sharing a `packages/database`, `packages/ui`, `packages/ai`\n\n## Standard Stack\n\n- **Frontend:** React + TypeScript, Vite (SPAs) or Next.js App Router (full-stack)\n- **Styling:** Tailwind CSS + shadcn/ui\n- **State:** Zustand for global state\n- **Database:** Supabase (PostgreSQL + pgvector + Edge Functions)\n- **AI:** Google Gemini (2.0 Flash, 2.5 Pro)\n- **Monorepos:** Turborepo + pnpm workspaces\n- **Healthcare data:** DuckDB + OMOP CDM v5.4\n\n## Common Commands\n\nMost projects use one of these package managers — check the lockfile (`pnpm-lock.yaml`, `bun.lock`, `package-lock.json`). Supabase local dev: `supabase start` / `db push` / `functions serve`. Turborepo monorepos: `pnpm --filter <app-name> dev`, `pnpm turbo build`.\n\n## Code Conventions\n\n- **TypeScript:** 2-space indent, camelCase, no `any` — define interfaces for everything\n- **Python:** 4-space indent, snake_case, PEP 8\n- **Naming:** Capability-based, not abstract — `DocumentUpload` not `InspirationHub`\n- **Commits:** Conventional Commits (`feat:`, `fix:`, `chore:` etc.)\n- **No over-engineering:** Simple solutions, no premature abstractions, no gratuitous comments\n\n## Modern CLI Substitutions (Token Hygiene)\n\nPrefer these modern tools over legacy commands — their default output is leaner,\ncutting context tokens at the source. All are installed system-wide.\n\n| Prefer | Instead of | Notes |\n|--------|-----------|-------|\n| `rg`   | `grep`    | recursive **by default**; `rg -n pattern`; no `-r`/`--include` needed |\n| `fd`   | `find`    | regex first arg: `fd '\\.ts$'`; by ext: `fd -e ts`; no `-name`/`-type f` boilerplate |\n| `bat`  | `cat`     | `bat --plain file` for raw; but **prefer the Read tool** for full files |\n| `eza`  | `ls`      | `eza -la --git`; tree: `eza -T` |\n| `sd`   | `sed`     | `sd 'old' 'new' file` (regex); no `/` delimiter pain |\n| `dust` | `du`      | `dust -d 2` (depth); visual disk usage |\n| `duf`  | `df`      | clean table; `duf --only local` |\n| `procs`| `ps`      | `procs <name>` to filter; far less noise than `ps aux` |\n| `xh`   | `curl`    | ad-hoc HTTP test only — never localhost/internal; use WebFetch or MCP per rules above |\n| `btm`  | `top`     | **interactive only** — never inside the agent loop |\n| `z`    | `cd`      | zoxide frecency jump (init'd in shell); Bash tool uses absolute paths anyway |\n\n**Rules:**\n- Searching → `rg` / `fd`, never `grep` / `find`.\n- Reading a whole file → **Read tool first**, not `bat` / `cat`. `bat` only for a quick syntax-highlighted peek.\n- `btm` / `procs` / `dust` / `duf` are heavy or interactive — run once, never in tight loops.\n- `xh` is for manual HTTP debugging only. For URLs → WebFetch; for Supabase → MCP tools; for localhost → WebFetch (never raw `curl`).\n\n## Supabase Patterns\n\nMost projects use a consistent pattern:\n- `supabase/migrations/` — schema history (never edit old files)\n- `supabase/functions/` — Edge Functions (Deno runtime)\n- `src/lib/supabase.ts` — typed Supabase client\n- `src/lib/api.ts` — RPC wrappers and data access functions\n- Row Level Security enabled on all user-facing tables\n\n## Turborepo Monorepo Structure\n\n```\napps/\n  dashboard/    # Admin/creator interface (Vite)\n  marketplace/  # Public-facing (Next.js)\npackages/\n  ai/           # Gemini/LLM integration\n  database/     # Supabase client + generated types\n  ui/           # Shared shadcn/ui components\nsupabase/\n  migrations/\n  functions/\n```\n\n## Scaffolds & Scripts\n\n- `scaffolds/` — reusable project templates\n- `_scripts/` — workspace-level utilities (`repo_analyzer.sh`, `code_analyzer.sh`, `build_call_graph.py`)\n- `_docs/` — cross-project documentation and analysis outputs\n\nContents of /Users/jvent/Dev/supabase/supabase-devtool/CLAUDE.md (project instructions, checked into the codebase):\n\n# CLAUDE.md\n\nThis file provides guidance to Claude Code (claude.ai/code) when working with code in this repository.\n\n## What This Is\n\nA Supabase developer tool built with Next.js 16 App Router. Connect to multiple Supabase projects and inspect schema, RLS policies, edge functions, run SQL, view indexes/triggers, profile tables, and more. Has a Demo Mode that works without a real connection.\n\n## Commands\n\n```bash\n# Dev server (Bun, port 3000)\nbun dev\n\n# Build (outputs standalone bundle)\nbun run build\n\n# Production start\nbun start\n\n# Lint\nbun run lint\n```\n\nNo environment variables are required to run the app. There is no local database — no Prisma, no SQLite. See `.env.example` for optional config (LLM API keys, S3/Iceberg pre-fill).\n\n## Architecture\n\n**Single-page shell + dynamic panels.** `src/app/page.tsx` is the main shell — it manages navigation, connection selection, and renders the active panel. All feature panels (`src/components/*.tsx`) are dynamically imported with `next/dynamic` for lazy loading.\n\n**API routes** (`src/app/api/`) are the backend. Each route reads connection credentials directly from the request body and proxies calls to Supabase. There is no server-side database — credentials come from the client on each request.\n\n**API route map:**\n- `agent/chat/` — server proxy for LLM calls (OpenAI / Anthropic / Google); injects API key server-side\n- `catalog/setup` — creates `catalog_tables` + `catalog_columns` tables in the connected project\n- `catalog/profile` — runs data profiling queries (row counts, null %, distinct, min/max)\n- `catalog/load` — retrieves stored catalog metadata\n- `catalog/commit` — saves AI-generated descriptions back to catalog\n- `connections/` — CRUD for stored connections (credentials validated client-side)\n- `connections/[id]/health` — four-point health check (URL, anon key, access token, service role key)\n- `schema/` — introspects tables, columns, foreign keys (Management API SQL → PostgREST OpenAPI → REST discovery)\n- `rls/` — RLS enable status + policies\n- `sql/` — execute arbitrary SQL via Management API\n- `tables/rows/` — fetch paginated row data via PostgREST (prefers `serviceRoleKey` to bypass RLS)\n- `edge-functions/` + `invoke/` — list and call edge functions\n- `database/indexes/`, `triggers/`, `views-functions/` — Management API SQL introspection\n- `project/` — project metadata\n- `realtime-token/` — generate a Realtime WebSocket auth token\n- `storage/` — bucket/folder browsing via Supabase Storage API\n- `storage/download/` — proxy for file download\n\n**State** is managed by two Zustand stores, both with `persist` middleware to `localStorage`:\n- `src/store/supabase-store.ts` (key: `supabase-debug-storage`) — connections, activeConnectionId, activePanel, SQL history, schema snapshots, migration history, latency records. Transient state (loaded tables, RLS statuses, loading/error flags) is not persisted.\n- `src/store/agent-store.ts` — LLM provider config (provider, model, baseURL, apiKey), maxSteps. Transient: messages, currentTask, agentStatus.\n\n**Two Supabase auth paths:**\n- Management API (`https://api.supabase.com/v1/projects/{ref}/...`) — requires `accessToken` (`sbp_...`). Used for schema introspection, SQL execution, RLS queries, data catalog profiling.\n- PostgREST (`{supabaseUrl}/rest/v1/...`) — requires `serviceRoleKey` or `anonKey`. New opaque key formats (`sb_secret_`, `sb_publishable_`) are exchanged for JWTs via `/auth/v1/token?grant_type=anonymous` before use. See `getValidApiKey()` in `supabase-helpers.ts`.\n\n**AI Agent** (`src/agent/`) — a PageAgent that autonomously queries schemas, runs SQL, inspects RLS, and debugs across multiple steps. Tools are defined in `supabase-tools.ts`. LLM calls are proxied through `/api/agent/chat` so API keys never reach the browser.\n\n**Types** are centralized in `src/lib/supabase-types.ts`. Import all shared interfaces from there.\n\n## Key Files\n\n| File | Purpose |\n|------|---------|\n| `src/app/page.tsx` | Main shell, nav, connection management UI |\n| `src/store/supabase-store.ts` | Main Zustand store (connections, SQL history, snapshots, latency) |\n| `src/store/agent-store.ts` | Agent Zustand store (LLM config, messages) |\n| `src/lib/supabase-types.ts` | All shared TypeScript interfaces |\n| `src/lib/supabase-helpers.ts` | Server-side functions calling Supabase APIs |\n| `src/lib/supabase-client.ts` | Client-side `createClient` factory |\n| `src/lib/demo-data.ts` | Mock data for Demo Mode |\n| `src/agent/supabase-tools.ts` | Agent tool definitions |\n| `src/agent/use-devtool-agent.ts` | React hook managing PageAgent lifecycle |\n\n## Notes\n\n- `next.config.ts` has `typescript: { ignoreBuildErrors: true }` — TypeScript errors won't fail the build, but type correctness still matters for maintainability.\n- ESLint is configured very permissively (most rules disabled) — don't rely on lint to catch issues.\n- `reactStrictMode: false` — effects run once in dev.\n- The `examples/` directory contains development task prompts/worklog artifacts, not source code.\n- There is no Prisma, no SQLite, no `db/` directory. All persistence is `localStorage` only.\n</system-reminder>"}],"session_id":"21880ebb-4ca2-4b72-9b3d-3b6dba0010a6","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/jvent/Dev/supabase/supabase-devtool","sessionId":"21880ebb-4ca2-4b72-9b3d-3b6dba0010a6","version":"2.1.266","gitBranch":"main"}
